\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b Types of Transformers\par
\par
\ulnone 1. Encoder-Only Transformers\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Use only the encoder part of the Transformer.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Good at understanding and analyzing text.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Used for classification, sentiment analysis, search, and question answering.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj Examples: BERT, RoBERTa, ALBERT\par
\par
\b 2. Decoder-Only Transformers\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Use only the decoder part.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Generate text one token at a time.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Mainly used for content generation and conversational AI.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj Examples: GPT-3, GPT-4, ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot\par
\par
\b 3. Encoder-Decoder Transformers\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Contain both encoder and decoder.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Encoder understands input, decoder generates output.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Best for translation, summarization, and text-to-text tasks.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj Examples: T5, BART, Original Transformer\par
\par
\b 4. Vision Transformers (ViT)\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Apply Transformer architecture to images.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Images are split into patches and treated like tokens.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Applications:\b0  Image classification, object detection\par
Examples: ViT, DeiT\par
\par
\b 5. Multimodal Transformers\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Process multiple data types together (text, images, audio, video).\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Enable AI systems to understand and generate across modalities.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj Examples: GPT-4o, Gemini, DALL\'b7E, Flamingo\par
\par
\b 6. Speech/Audio Transformers\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Designed for speech recognition and audio generation.\par
\f3\fs20\'b7\tab\f1\fs24 Handle audio sequences similarly to text tokens.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj Examples: Whisper, SpeechT5\par
\par
\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qc\ul\b\fs44 Pre-Training Vs Fine-Tuning\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\fs28 Pre-Training\par
\ulnone\b0\fs24 Pretraining is the initial training phase where an AI model learns general knowledge from massive datasets such as books, websites, articles, and code repositories.\line It learns language patterns, grammar, facts, reasoning, and relationships between words.\line The model is typically trained using tasks like next-word prediction.\line Pretraining requires enormous computational resources and large amounts of data.\line The output is a foundation model (e.g., GPT, BERT) that can later be adapted through fine-tuning for specific tasks.\par
\par
\ul\b\fs28 Fine-Tuning\par
\ulnone\b0\fs24 Fine-Tuning is the process of adapting a pretrained AI model to a specific task or domain using specialized data.\line It helps the model perform better in areas such as healthcare, finance, legal services, or customer support.\line Fine-tuning requires much less data and computing power than pretraining.\line During this process, the model's parameters are adjusted to learn domain-specific knowledge.\line The result is a more accurate and specialized AI model for a particular application.\par
\par
\par
\par
